    <DemoExample Title="Validation" RazorCode="@example20RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example20CsharpCode" Id="example20">
        <div class="example-desc">
            Inside an <b>EditForm</b> the DateRangePicker participates in the data annotations validation like any other
            input: the field is marked invalid until the bound <b>BitDateRangePickerValue</b> satisfies the model's rules,
            and the messages are reported through <b>ValidationMessage</b>. A custom validation attribute is the natural
            place for range rules that the calendar itself cannot express.
            <br /><br />
            A range that reaches the component as text is checked against the calendar's own restrictions as well, and
            the three ways it can fail each have their own message: <b>InvalidErrorMessage</b> for text that cannot be
            read as a range, <b>DisabledDateErrorMessage</b> for a range that lands on a day the application has
            blocked, and <b>OutOfRangeErrorMessage</b> for one that breaks the bounds or the min/max length.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <EditForm Model="validationModel" OnValidSubmit="HandleValidSubmit" OnInvalidSubmit="HandleInvalidSubmit">
                <DataAnnotationsValidator />

                <BitDateRangePicker @bind-Value="validationModel.DateRange" />

                <ValidationMessage For="@(() => validationModel.DateRange)" />
                <br />
                <BitButton ButtonType="BitButtonType.Submit">Submit</BitButton>
                <BitButton ButtonType="BitButtonType.Reset" Variant="BitVariant.Outline"
                           OnClick="() => { validationModel = new(); successMessage = string.Empty; }">
                    Reset
                </BitButton>
            </EditForm>
            <br />
            @if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(successMessage) is false)
            {
                <BitMessage Color="BitColor.Success">@successMessage</BitMessage>
            }
        </div>
        <br /><br /><br />
        <div>Type a weekend into the field below to see the blocked-day message, and a range longer than 7 days to see the out-of-range one:</div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitDateRangePicker Label="Custom messages"
                                AllowTextInput
                                DateFormat="dd/MM/yyyy"
                                ValueFormat="{0} - {1}"
                                MaxRange="TimeSpan.FromDays(7)"
                                DisabledDaysOfWeek="@([DayOfWeek.Saturday, DayOfWeek.Sunday])"
                                InvalidErrorMessage="Write the range as dd/MM/yyyy - dd/MM/yyyy."
                                DisabledDateErrorMessage="Weekends are not bookable."
                                OutOfRangeErrorMessage="A booking cannot be longer than a week."
                                Placeholder="dd/MM/yyyy - dd/MM/yyyy" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Accessibility" RazorCode="@example21RazorCode" Id="example21">
        <div class="example-desc">
            The field is a <code>combobox</code> that owns a modal <code>dialog</code>, and the calendar inside it is a
            <code>grid</code>: the whole grid is one tab stop and the arrow keys move within it, so no keyboard user has
            to tab past six weeks of days. The focus enters the callout when it opens, stays inside it while it is open
            and returns to the field when it closes.
            <br /><br />
            <b>In the day grid:</b> arrow keys move by a day or a week, <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd> to the ends of the
            week, <kbd>PageUp</kbd>/<kbd>PageDown</kbd> by a month (with <kbd>Shift</kbd>, by a year),
            <kbd>Enter</kbd>/<kbd>Space</kbd> picks, <kbd>Escape</kbd> closes. Disabled days are skipped rather than
            landed on. The month and year grids answer the same keys, four cells to a row.
            <b>In the field:</b> <kbd>ArrowDown</kbd> opens the calendar. <b>In the time picker:</b>
            <kbd>PageUp</kbd>/<kbd>PageDown</kbd> step by <b>HourStep</b> and <b>MinuteStep</b>.
            <br /><br />
            While the start date is picked and the end date is not, the arrow keys paint the range they would produce
            exactly as the pointer does. Every cell and button of the calendar draws the same focus ring, in the accent
            of <b>Color</b>; Windows High Contrast re-establishes the states that are otherwise a background - the range,
            its preview, today, the highlighted days - as borders and system colors; and the callout's entry animation
            collapses under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.
            <br /><br />
            Every control carries a name of its own, and all of them are parameters, so the whole component localizes
            without a resource file: <b>CalloutAriaLabel</b>, <b>PresetsAriaLabel</b>, the <b>GoTo…Title</b> and
            <b>…ButtonTitle</b> texts, the time picker's <b>…AriaLabel</b> texts, and <b>SelectedDateAriaAtomic</b>,
            the template of the live region that announces the range as it is built. <b>AriaLabel</b> names the field
            itself where no visible <b>Label</b> does.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitDateRangePicker AriaLabel="Stay dates"
                                Placeholder="No visible label, named for screen readers"
                                ShowClearButton
                                ShowTimePicker
                                CalloutAriaLabel="Stay dates calendar"
                                ClearButtonTitle="Clear the stay dates"
                                GoToTodayTitle="Jump to today"
                                GoToPrevMonthTitle="Previous month"
                                GoToNextMonthTitle="Next month"
                                SelectedDateAriaAtomic="Stay selected: {0}"
                                StartTimeHourInputAriaLabel="Check-in hour"
                                StartTimeMinuteInputAriaLabel="Check-in minute"
                                EndTimeHourInputAriaLabel="Check-out hour"
                                EndTimeMinuteInputAriaLabel="Check-out minute" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Open state" RazorCode="@example22RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example22CsharpCode" Id="example22">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>IsOpen</b> is the open state of the callout and it is two-way bound, so the calendar can be opened and
            closed from anywhere on the page as well as from the field. The same two moves are available on a component
            reference as <b>OpenCallout</b> and <b>CloseCallout</b>.
            <br /><br />
            Bound one way only - an <b>IsOpen</b> the picker has no way to write back to - the picker stops closing
            itself: the second pick still goes through, but the callout waits for the page to close it.
            <b>OnOpen</b> and <b>OnClose</b> report every opening and closing whichever of the two caused it.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitButton OnClick="() => isOpen = true">Open the calendar</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            <BitDateRangePicker Label="Two-way bound IsOpen" @bind-IsOpen="isOpen" @bind-Value="isOpenValue" />
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>The callout is <b>@(isOpen ? "open" : "closed")</b></div>
        <br /><br /><br />
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="OpenFromCode">OpenCallout()</BitButton>
            <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="CloseFromCode">CloseCallout()</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            <BitDateRangePicker @ref="calloutPicker" Label="Driven by its component reference" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example23RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example23CsharpCode" Id="example23">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>BitParams</b> carries a <b>BitDateRangePickerParams</b> down to every picker under it, so a booking form or
            a whole page settles the shared shape of its pickers - the culture, the bounds, the presets, the look - once
            instead of on every one of them. What it carries is a default and not an override: a picker that writes a
            parameter for itself keeps its own value and only what it left unset is filled in from the cascade, which is
            what lets one picker step out of the group without the group having to know about it. Every parameter of the
            component is on the params object, and each is filled in on its own.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitParams Parameters="@dateRangePickerParams">
                <BitDateRangePicker Label="Departure &amp; return" />
                <br /><br />
                <BitDateRangePicker Label="Second trip" />
                <br /><br />
                <BitDateRangePicker Label="One month of its own, and no presets" MonthCount="1" Presets="@(Array.Empty<BitDateRangePickerPreset>())" />
            </BitParams>
        </div>
        <br /><br /><br />
        <div>Outside the cascade:</div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitDateRangePicker Label="Untouched by it" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

